    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Luba Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upemba-depression">Upemba Depression</a> in what is now south-eastern DR Congo.</li><li>Its model of royal power, checked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambudye">Bambudye</a> society, was copied by neighbours such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a> across the southern savanna.</li><li>A succession war after c. 1870, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> conquest in the 1890s ended its independence.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Luba historians kept royal memory on the lukasa, a hand-held beaded board whose patterns of beads and shells helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambudye">Bambudye</a> adepts recall kings, places and lineages.</p></aside>
    </section>

